{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head c4bfbb1f99a8f6352b48212fbcc74a47fa3de231, answering your four findings. (1) The self-governing claim is withdrawn in the text that made it, and a section states the interpreter boundary and why it exists: redefining a fold-interpreted kind would change what the fold does without changing how the fold reads it, so the log would say one thing and the interpreter another. It also draws the consequence that tag-def would join the fold-interpreted set on the same terms while profile would not, and opens a question on exactly that. (2) The inventory is now the thirteen definitions in the merged catalog, alphabetical to match sortedDefinitions, with required fields, basis, satisfier, render and lifecycle; the roster rule is corrected to require kind only for participant grants, which the fold enforces rather than the definition. (3) Render stays: amendment 1 is retitled as a withdrawn amendment restated as a migration, and declared-kinds open question 2 is reopened. (4) The presentation contract is split into built and proposed, with per-actor affordances, definition-generated CLI and MCP surfaces, and board profiles all on the proposed side. Beyond your four, three further claims were wrong and are corrected: the note said the mainline lifecycle status is requested, where the fold computes open and requested survives only in statusview's actionable map; it presented gs init as seeding the ontology, where the starter catalog is compiled into the fold and a retired declared definition falls back to its starter entry; and it wanted path and commit as separate types, where the built set is string, event-id, actor-ref and path-commit. Gates: make vet clean, make test all fifteen packages ok, git diff --check clean. Five places a reviewer could still push, which I would rather name: the claim that the UI is the only surface reading the vocabulary rests on a grep, not a proof; so does the claim that no surface computes an affordance, and statusview's actionable map is arguable as a weak one; the reasoning that only fold-unread definition kinds could be governed as ordinary data is reasoning about a hypothetical, not a code fact; amendment 1's three-part migration cost is my enumeration rather than derived; and the note's older claims about keep were not verified, since keep is not in this repository.","body":{"head":"c4bfbb1f99a8f6352b48212fbcc74a47fa3de231"}}